  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Thursday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Cold temperatures aloft and minimal\nbuoyancy should support isolated thunderstorm formation in parts\nof Eastern Nevada this afternoon. The primary thunderstorm hazards\nwill be wind gusts near 45 mph and dangerous cloud to ground\nlightning strikes. An Eastern Pacific storm system will dig\nsouthward tonight, before making landfall over Northern California\nSaturday. This low pressure system will move eastward through\nNorthern and Central Nevada Saturday night and Sunday, resulting\nin periods of valley rain and mountain snow. The majority of\nprecipitation will fall Saturday night and Sunday morning. Snow \nlevels will start at around 8,000 feet this afternoon, before \ndropping to about 6,500 feet Sunday morning. Here are\nprobabilities of one inch or more of rain for select locations in\nNorthern and Central Nevada from 5 PM PDT Friday, October 30th,\n2026 through 4 AM PST Monday, November 2nd, 2026: \n\nLund - 89%\nLamoille - 73%\nJiggs - 72%\nEly - 67% \nSpring Creek - 66%\nAustin - 65% \nDuckwater - 64%\nWells - 60% \nElko - 57%\nEureka - 47%\n\nPrecipitation will come to an end early Monday morning at the\nlatest. A cooling trend is expected this weekend. By Sunday\nafternoon, high temperatures throughout Northern and Central\nNevada will be eleven to thirteen degrees below normal for this\ntime of year. Dry weather is anticipated Monday afternoon through\nat least Thursday morning. A warming trend is expected Monday and\nTuesday. By Tuesday afternoon, maximum temperatures across\nNorthern and Central Nevada will be near seasonal values.\nHigh temperatures will remain near seasonal values both Wednesday\nafternoon and Thursday afternoon. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High forecast confidence\nregarding periods of valley rain and mountain snow this afternoon\nthrough Sunday night. Moderate forecast confidence in the\npossibility of a few strong thunderstorms both this afternoon and\nSaturday afternoon. Moderate forecast confidence regarding a\nmarginal risk of excessive rainfall Saturday afternoon through\nSunday morning. High forecast confidence in a cooling trend this\nweekend. No deviations from the NBM model output.",
